{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"MISTAKES I MADE — recorded because the pattern repeats and the next agent should expect it.\n\n1. TESTS THAT PASS FOR THE WRONG REASON — three times.\n   a. Claimed twice that experiment had REFUTED a reviewer finding that run_repair self-deadlocks. Both probes ran against the synthetic graph-test tracker, which is NOT a git repo, so stage_and_commit bails before it ever takes the write lock and rev-parse HEAD returns empty. Every assertion compared \"\" to \"\". Against a REAL git-backed tracker: repair_finding without the outer lock = repaired in 0.1s; run_repair holding it = unrepairable after 60.0s on a flock timeout. The reviewer was right; I was wrong twice and had over-weighted my own null result against the live evidence (zero progress in 10 minutes was always more consistent with blocking than with slow pushes).\n   b. The pre-repair tag test was tautological twice over: HEAD never moved between runs, and `git rev-parse` of an ABSENT ref echoes the ref name to stdout rather than returning empty, so the \"tag must not exist\" check passed on a non-empty string.\n   c. On fec5, an earlier test draft stubbed rebar.link wholesale, so a mutation that swallowed the value in _python_leaf PASSED when it should have failed — through the exact layer the review had flagged.\n   LESSON: a mutation check is only as good as the fixture. Verify the probe can detect the OPPOSITE before trusting a null result.\n\n2. FILED A P1 BUG ON A MISREAD (480e, closed not_a_bug). Grepped for an import line, saw that same import fail in a bare interpreter, concluded the reconciler in-flight probe could never read the lock — without reading the four lines ABOVE it that insert engine_dir() into sys.path. The guard was correct; a reconciler pass really was in flight. /rebar-debug then proved it properly: 12 samples of a frozen heartbeat, then check_pass_lock returning False once the lease expired.\n\n3. ASSERTED A PROPERTY THE SYSTEM DID NOT HAVE. Wrote in the story that the redirect record is \"kept as the loud signal\". True only on the CLI path; rebar.link() discards it. Became ticket fec5.\n\n4. PRESENTED A CACHED RESULT AS A FRESH ONE. Reported that fec5 \"passed cleanly under stricter task scoring\" — it had not run at all (runner: reused). Operator caught it. The forced re-run returned BLOCK with 3 findings, including that the planned fix would have been a NO-OP because three layers dropped the return value. Became ticket 6e4f. USE --force when a re-review must actually re-run.\n\n5. Destroyed my own composer.py edits with a `git checkout` during mutation cleanup; caught by a grep guard before commit."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785448768291806001,"uuid":"fe869288-387a-473c-837c-d00bd85e24db"}